WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.809 --> 00:00:06.415
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.415 --> 00:00:11.107
Hi, my name is Amir Goldberg, and I'm
a Professor of Organizational Behavior at

00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:15.323
the Stanford Graduate School of Business,
where I teach a class titled,

00:00:15.323 --> 00:00:16.486
People Analytics.

00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:20.391
People analytics is the application
of data analytics methods,

00:00:20.391 --> 00:00:23.089
especially machine learning algorithms for

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:27.783
the purpose of informing people
related-decisions inside organizations.

00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:30.339
Here are five takeaways from that class.

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:34.311
[MUSIC]

00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:39.047
Data are revolutionizing how people
are managing inside organizations.

00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:43.315
Using data analytics doesn't mean
that you need to be a data analyst.

00:00:43.315 --> 00:00:48.835
What you need to be is someone who
understands what data analytics can and

00:00:48.835 --> 00:00:49.747
cannot do.

00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:54.423
And most importantly, understand how to
interpret data analytics in terms of what

00:00:54.423 --> 00:00:57.920
it means about what's happening
inside your organization.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.715
[MUSIC]

00:01:02.715 --> 00:01:07.255
It's really important for
you to understand what algorithms can or

00:01:07.255 --> 00:01:08.057
cannot do.

00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:10.762
And people call it
artificial intelligence but

00:01:10.762 --> 00:01:14.013
machines are not intelligent
in the way that humans are.

00:01:14.013 --> 00:01:15.343
What are they good at?

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:19.105
Prediction, by which I mean
looking at historical data and

00:01:19.105 --> 00:01:21.898
then making predictions about the future.

00:01:21.898 --> 00:01:28.447
Your role is to use those predictions
as a means to inform your decisions.

00:01:28.447 --> 00:01:31.131
It is you who makes the decisions.

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:34.108
It's the machines who
make the predictions.

00:01:34.108 --> 00:01:37.539
[MUSIC]

00:01:37.539 --> 00:01:40.314
There's a lot of snake oil out there.

00:01:40.314 --> 00:01:45.045
If it sounds fantastical,
it's probably no good.

00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:48.398
It's very difficult to
predict human behavior.

00:01:48.398 --> 00:01:51.569
The most important thing for you to ask,

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:57.084
is this prediction predicting
an outcome that I actually care about?

00:01:57.084 --> 00:02:01.155
If the person who developed the algorithm
can demonstrate to you that it moves

00:02:01.155 --> 00:02:05.549
the needle on outcomes that are important
for you, then it's worth considering.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:08.305
But if they can't,
you should probably take a pass.

00:02:08.305 --> 00:02:13.568
[MUSIC]

00:02:13.568 --> 00:02:17.799
When it comes to making decisions about
other humans, all of us are bias.

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:21.102
We pay attention to other people's race,
to their gender,

00:02:21.102 --> 00:02:23.837
to their physical appearance,
to their accent.

00:02:23.837 --> 00:02:28.297
And those are often entirely irrelevant
to the decisions that we need to make.

00:02:28.297 --> 00:02:34.660
So use your algorithms in a way that
would help you overcome those biases and

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.601
make your decisions better and
more ethical.

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:41.948
[MUSIC]

00:02:41.948 --> 00:02:44.853
When it comes to making
decisions about other people,

00:02:44.853 --> 00:02:47.006
those can be immensely consequential.

00:02:47.006 --> 00:02:50.565
They can relate to their livelihood,
to their sense of worth,

00:02:50.565 --> 00:02:52.659
to their psychological well being.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:54.704
We can't take those lightly.

00:02:54.704 --> 00:02:58.888
And it's really important that
when you make these decisions,

00:02:58.888 --> 00:03:03.459
you don't hide behind machines, nor
do you outsource the morality of

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:06.886
the decision to a machine
learning decision maker.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:09.755
Ultimately, it's your responsibility.

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:14.841
Machines are neither moral or immoral,
they do what you tell them to.

00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:20.784
And it's important that you think about
the moral implications of your decisions.

00:03:20.784 --> 00:03:27.435
The good news, ethical decisions or also
better managerial decisions, good luck.

00:03:27.435 --> 00:03:37.435
[MUSIC]

00:03:41.335 --> 00:03:46.445
My mom once sat in the class and
at the end of the class she said to me,

00:03:46.445 --> 00:03:50.756
Amir, I have no idea what you said but
you're a genius.

00:03:50.756 --> 00:03:54.594
Whereas my dad said,
that was very interesting, but

00:03:54.594 --> 00:03:57.762
you didn't teach them anything, did you?

00:03:57.762 --> 00:04:02.088
So that gives you a good kind of summary
of how my parents took the class.

00:04:02.088 --> 00:04:12.088
[MUSIC]

